---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LETTERS, HOLY_NAMES, MOTHERS, DOUBLES } from '../lib/transits.js';
import { SIGN_ICONS } from '../lib/icons-signs.js';
import { ORGAN_ICONS } from '../lib/icons-organs.js';

const MONTHS = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May', 'Jun', 'Jul', 'Aug', 'Sep', 'Oct', 'Nov', 'Dec'];
const rangeOf = (l) => `${MONTHS[l.start[0] - 1]} ${l.start[1]} – ${MONTHS[l.end[0] - 1]} ${l.end[1]}`;
---

<BaseLayout title="How to practice">
  <h1>How to practice</h1>

  <p>
    Picture a letter not as ink on a page, but as something alive standing in front of you.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is where this practice begins. Every Hebrew letter is a living entity — a version
    of you. Not a symbol to memorize. A presence to sit with.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have never done anything like this before, good. You need nothing to start.
    No background, no prior study. Just a letter, a breath, and a few quiet minutes.
  </p>

  <p class="dim small">
    A note before anything else: the correspondences below come from Sefer Yetzirah,
    chapters 3, 4 and 5. The practice itself — how the letters are
    used here, and what they open up — is my own experience, not official teaching.
  </p>

  <h2>The daily practice, in three parts</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>By day, the sun's letter.</strong> The sun moves through one sign each month,
    and each sign carries a letter. That letter is yours for the day. Carry it the way you
    would carry a friend's name in your pocket — recite it while you walk, while you work,
    while you wait. You will find it on the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`}>today's letters</a> page
    each morning.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>By night, the moon's letter.</strong> When evening comes, the moon has its own
    sign, and its own letter. This one is for the quiet hours. Sit with it before sleep,
    the way you would sit by a window and watch the night settle.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Through both, the day's holy name.</strong> Each weekday carries a name of its
    own — El on Sunday, Elohim on Monday, YHWH on Tuesday, YHWH Tzevaot on Wednesday,
    Elohim Tzevaot on Thursday, Shaddai on Friday, Adonai on Shabbat. Hold it in the mind
    and heart. Never spoken aloud with the mouth — only thought, quietly, inside.
  </p>

  <h2>How to sit with a letter</h2>

  <p>
    Find a quiet chair. Sit the way you would sit with an old friend — no hurry,
    nowhere else to be.
  </p>
  <p>
    Breathe. Slow, silent breath, the way Alef breathes. Alef is the breath itself —
    no sound, no shape, just the air moving in and out. Let that be the pace.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now recite the letter. Stay with it a while. Do not rush to the next thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each letter makes an organ, and the organs hold memories — good and bad. As you
    recite, notice what comes. A place in the body. A person from years ago. An energy
    you forgot you carried. In my own practice, reciting brought back a physiotherapist
    I met ten, twelve years ago, and time spent with my grandfather. The organs cry that
    they are alive, and these are our memories.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not chase the memory. Do not push it away either. Let it pass through like a
    scene in a film — it arrives, it plays, it goes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The letter is a tool, not an idol. The point is the Creator himself — not power,
    wealth, or status from the letters. The moment a letter becomes something you worship
    for what it can give you, it has stopped being a tool.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sefirot points connect the organs and make the entity one. We are letters, and
    each letter has its own memory — as our body parts, and as our main sefirot points.
  </p>

  <h2>The 22 letters, in three groups</h2>

  <p>
    There are 22 letters, and they come in a set order. First the 3 mothers.
    Then the 7 doubles. Then the 12 commons, last. That order is the manner
    of it — do not skip ahead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each letter is a practice on its own. Pick one and sit with it, alone,
    as you like. A practice holds the month's letter, the day's letter, and
    the moon's letter. For the verse connected to it, see the
    <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}prayer/`}>prayer</a> section.
  </p>

  <h2>The three mothers</h2>

  <table class="mothers-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Letter</th><th>Element</th><th>Place</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {MOTHERS.map((m) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="hebrew">{m.glyph} {m.pointed} — {m.name}</td>
          <td>{m.element}</td>
          <td>{m.place}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="mothers-cards">
    {MOTHERS.map((m) => (
      <article class="letter-card">
        <p class="card-letter">{m.glyph} {m.pointed} — {m.name}</p>
        <p>{m.element}</p>
        <p class="dim">{m.place}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>The seven days</h2>

  <table class="doubles-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Day</th><th>Letter</th><th>Planet</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {DOUBLES.map((d) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{d.day}</td>
          <td class="hebrew">{d.glyph} {d.pointed} — {d.name}</td>
          <td>{d.planet}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="doubles-cards">
    {DOUBLES.map((d) => (
      <article class="letter-card">
        <p class="dim small">{d.day}</p>
        <p class="card-letter">{d.glyph} {d.pointed} — {d.name}</p>
        <p class="dim">{d.planet}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>The twelve letters</h2>

  <table class="letters-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Letter</th><th>Sign</th><th>Organ</th><th>Sense</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {LETTERS.map((l) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="hebrew">{l.glyph} {l.pointed} — {l.name}</td>
          <td><span class="sign-icon" style={`--icon-src: url(${SIGN_ICONS[l.sign]})`} aria-hidden="true"></span>{l.sign}<div class="dim small">{rangeOf(l)}</div></td>
          <td><span class="organ-icon" style={`--icon-src: url(${ORGAN_ICONS[l.organ]})`} aria-hidden="true"></span>{l.organ}</td>
          <td>{l.sense}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="letters-cards">
    {LETTERS.map((l) => (
      <article class="letter-card">
        <p class="card-letter">{l.glyph} {l.pointed} — {l.name}</p>
        <p><span class="sign-icon" style={`--icon-src: url(${SIGN_ICONS[l.sign]})`} aria-hidden="true"></span>{l.sign}</p>
        <p class="dim small">{rangeOf(l)}</p>
        <p><span class="organ-icon" style={`--icon-src: url(${ORGAN_ICONS[l.organ]})`} aria-hidden="true"></span>{l.organ}</p>
        <p class="dim">{l.sense}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>The holy names of the week</h2>

  <table class="holy-names-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Day</th><th>Name</th><th>Sefirah</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {HOLY_NAMES.map((h) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{h.day}</td>
          <td class="hebrew">{h.pointed} — {h.name}</td>
          <td>{h.sefirah}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="holy-names-cards">
    {HOLY_NAMES.map((h) => (
      <article class="letter-card">
        <p class="dim small">{h.day}</p>
        <p class="card-letter">{h.pointed} — {h.name}</p>
        <p class="dim">{h.sefirah}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
